 <iati-activity default-currency="EUR" hierarchy="2" humanitarian="0" iati-activities:version="2.02" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <iati-identifier>NL-KVK-40409352-PRJ13-220-0013</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-40409352" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Long term data and digital support to long-term partners</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Geographical focus on MENA and Africa Region but not limited</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IFRC
NS (significant needs, may change over time): Burundi, Madagascar, Nigeria
NS (medium needs, may change over time): Djibouti, Rwanda, Sierra Leone, South Africa, Botswana, Syria, Chad, Eswatini</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In general, the digitalization of CVA processes faces challenges, with limited senior management support affecting decision-making on investing in CVA-IM. CVA staff, recognizing the need for digitalization, grapple with limited knowledge about existing tools and how to adapt them to local contexts. Basic data literacy skills are found lacking across National Societies, and at the same time, capacity building remains a challenge as often there is no team to ensure the knowledge stays within the organization during the staff turnover. Therefore it is important to ensure that knowledge is built wider within the organization, to have a larger group of people with IM capacities and to preserve operational continuity</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">There are several challenges that justify the need to integrate relevant and adequate IM into CVA interventions. The recurring distribution cycles, leading to extensive data accumulation, necessitate robust data management processes. Additionally, the massive data collection requires strong deduplication procedures. In turn, the integration of digital payment services, as mobile money and digital bank accounts, offers streamlined processes but concurrently raises concerns about data responsibility and safeguarding personally identifiable information.

The urgency to address these challenges aligns with the IFRC objective to deliver 50% of humanitarian assistance through CVA by 2025. The concerns highlighted in the IFRC 2030 Strategy are valid, pointing to insufficient technical skills and knowledge within the RCRC network, including training, development, sourcing, and retention of the right skill sets</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x2022;	Advocacy for better CVA-IM in CVA implementations
&#x2022;	Support to IFRC CVA Global team
&#x2022;	Support CVA-IM Preparedness assessments
&#x2022;	Trainings in CVA-IM systems and IM oriented to CVA
&#x2022;	Operational support to NSs</narrative>
  </description>
